Image SXM history        v1.59        ( Jan 96 - Mar 96 )____________________________________________________________________________________v1.59    ·2    ( 27 Mar 96 )Added features: ¥ The compensation routines (available from the SPM/Compensation submenu) that were written for loading images from 16-bit data files, have been modified to work on 8-bit images. If a compensation item is selected for an SPM image, then the original (raw) 16-bit data will be loaded from the image file and used to generate the new image. If the image is not an SPM image (ie, an image loaded from a PICT or TIFF file that may, or may not, have originated from an SPM image) then the 8-bit data used to display the image in the window will be used to generate the new image. Applying the compensation routines to the smaller range of Z values in an 8-bit image, compared to the raw 16-bit data, will result in lower image quality, but has the advantage of allowing various compensation modes to be applied sequentially if desired. ¥ The content of a Profile Plot window can now be duplicated into an image window using the standard 'Duplicate' menu item or keyboard shortcut cmd-D. This allows, for instance, many different profiles to be displayed, stacked, montaged, etc. ¥ Substantial changes have been made to the way that the Profile Plot command works, controlled by check boxes added to the 'Profile Plot Options' dialog box. If the 'Colour Profile Plots' box is checked, then the profile will be drawn in the current foreground colour as indicated by the colour of the brush in the Tool window. (Note: Up to 6 colours can be added to any LUT from the 'LUT Options...' dialog box.) If the 'Add RGB Colours to LUT' box is checked, then the three primary colours will be added to the current LUT and the foreground colour set to red by default. The profile will be updated dynamically as the line selection is moved around the image, either by dragging with the mouse or by nudging using the arrow keys. The profile can be frozen by pressing the CapsLock key. Pressing the Option key changes the 'Plot Profile' menu item to 'Overlay Profile' and a second profile will be drawn without erasing the existing profile. This also will be updated dynamically. If the foreground colour selected for the overlay profile is the same as that used for the original profile, then an alternative colour will be selected automatically. If the 'Add Grey Border' box is checked, then a light grey border and drop shadow are added to the display. ¥ Image SXM can now read Omicron AFM friction images (the default filename identifier is 'tb'). This has been tested on only a limited number of images. ¥ Image SXM can now read old-format WA Technology STM images (pre-TOPSystemª). This has been tested on only a limited number of images.Bug fixes: ¥ The precision with which Profile Plot values were exported was sometimes incorrect (see below for other changes).  ¥ The compensation displayed by the 'Get Info' command now reads 'none' for SXM images read from 8-bit data files, such as JEOL SEM and old-format WA Technology SPM images. Other changes: ¥ The maximum width of an image has been reset to the NIH Image default of 4096 pixels (after being reduced to 2048 pixels previously due to problems allocating memory to certain variables). The maximum width of image that can be used to produce a 3d Plot involving shadow calculations has been increased from 640 to 1024. ¥ Exporting Profile Plot values now produces two columns of data for the position (X) and intensity (Z) values. The precision with which the data are exported is always one decimal place more than that used for displaying the values in the Info window. If this is not sufficient, then the number of decimal places can be specified in the 'Options...' dialog box in the Analyze menu. ¥ Changing the Zoom window magnification from x2 to x4 (tab-click or tab-shift-click) or vice versa no longer requires the image window to be in front of the Zoom window.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.59    ·1    ( 9 Feb 96 )Added features: ¥ Image SXM will now recognise Nanoscope III v4.1 images that record Amplitude, Current, Deflection, Frequency, Friction, Height or Phase. The Z scale calibration has been checked against a set of standard images and is OK.Bug fixes: ¥ The crash that occurred after cancelling 'List Params from File(s)...' has been fixed. ¥ The display of Z values when the cursor was in the LUT window had the wrong units. ¥ The Balloon Help text was wrong in the Analyse/Options dialog box. Now OK. ¥ The Balloon Help text is wrong in Preferences dialog box. This a bug in NIH Image which is a bit tricky for me to fix as the text is embedded inside code where I can't edit it. I have left it for NIH Image v1.60 to fix. ¥ Setting a custom LUT, saving the Preferences and then launching Image SXM caused unpredictable behaviour on FPU Macs. This bug has been fixed. ¥ Browsing would crash if an alias was found in the folder. Aliases are now ignored. ¥ The display of XY values in the Info window when the cursor is in the Plot window now always shows the correct XY values and units, even if original image from which the plot was taken has been closed or other images have been opened subsequently. ¥ Changing the foreground or background colour (from the default black and white, respectively) of an image and then saving the Preferences file would result in Image SXM crashing the next time it was launched. This has been fixed.Other changes: ¥ Stacks now have independent Z scales for each slice in the stack. This is of importance for Nanoscope III files which contain multiple images, where each image may have different Z scales and units.  ¥ Display precision for XY values is now based on units (, nm, µm) per pixel, rather than image size. ¥ Similarly, Z precision is based on units (nm, nA, V, Hz,...) per Z value. ¥ Extra parameters are displayed in the 'Get Info' window for Nanoscope III images. ¥ The Star Trek sound bites have gone! It's a pity, but it does result in a slightly smaller application. ¥ Line-by-line least-squares fit subtraction can now be done using a 0th, 1st or 2nd order fit. ¥ Balloon Help has been added to the 'Display Calib Bar...', 'Browse Images...' and 'Browse Omicron...' dialog boxes. ¥ All SXM-related dialog boxes now have a Help button (indicated by a '?') which toggles Balloon Help on and off. If Balloon Help has been activated using this button, it will be deactivated automatically when the dialog box is closed. If Balloon Help is activated from the menu bar, it remains active until cancelled.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.59    §2    ( 24 Jan 96 )    Beta releaseAdded features: ¥ A new SPM/Compensation submenu has been added. In addition to the XY Tilt Compensation present in earlier versions, compensation for large-scale curvature of SPM surfaces and a line-by-line least-squares fit subtraction are available. The latter can be applied over a specified region of the image (not necessarily rectangular). The ReCompensation command has been left in the SPM/Images submenu for compatibility with earlier versions. ¥ A new SPM/Dimensions submenu has been added to allow the XYZ units displayed in the Info and Results windows to be changed from the values set by default on loading. The changes can apply to all open images or only the frontmost image according to a checkbox setting in the SPM Options dialog box. ¥ Image SXM can now read JEOL SEM images that do not originate on a WinSemª system. Bug fixes: ¥ NIH Image v1.59 fixes a number of bugs present in v1.58. See the NIH Image documentation for details. ¥ For Digital Instruments Nanoscope III v3 and v4.1, the Z scale is now read from the file header correctly. ¥ The display of step heights in 'Calc Step Height' gave the correct values but the wrong units. This has been fixed. ¥ The display of Z values when the cursor is in a Histogram window gave the correct values but the wrong units. This has been fixed. Not related to the bug mentioned above, surprisingly. ¥ PICT and TIFF images displayed Z scale values but not the units. This has been fixed. ¥ Some TIFF images gave a spurious 'Invalid TIFF header' message. This has been fixed. ¥ The 'OpenAll' checkbox in the 'Open...' dialog box now works on SXM images.Other changes: ¥ A new FFT submenu was introduced in NIH Image v1.59. The FFT menu in earlier versions of Image SXM has therefore been removed to maintain compatibility with NIH Image. See the NIH Image documentation for details. ¥ When the cursor is in an FFT window, the Info window displays frequency, period and polar angle as well as the XY coordinates. ¥ The FFT command selects the largest power-of-two-sized square if no other selection has been made. ¥ Up to 10 images can be loaded from one Digital Instruments Nanoscope III file. This feature is untested. ¥ The calculation of Phase% now displays percentage values below the lower slice limit and above the higher slice limit, and the continuous display no longer flickers as the slice limits are varied. ¥ The reference number of the folder containing the file from which an image is loaded is now stored for each image. Thus, if Image SXM needs to read the raw data of an SPM image, it should know where to find it. ¥ Cmd -> and cmd <- now load the next and previous images in numerical sequence for Digital Instruments Nanoscope and Park Scientific SPM images - previously this worked only for old-format Omicron STM images. ¥ The 'Duplicate' command now adjusts the SPM parameters relating to the image size. ¥ 'Print Parameters for File(s)...' is now 'List Parameters for File(s)...', producing a list of parameters in a text window which can then be printed if desired. It now works for any SPM image -  previously this worked only for old-format Omicron STM images ¥ A number of cosmetic changes have been made: 'STM' is now 'SPM' where a feature is equally applicable to STM and SFM images; the SXM menu has been removed; SAM Options is in the SEM menu; SXM Options and 3d Plot are in the SPM menu; the Resize Window submenu is in the Windows menu.____________________________________________________________________________________